Notebook[{
Cell["PadicNormalize", "ObjectName",ExpressionUUID->"f0a9522c-0c0c-46cd-a342-9c17960c3927"],

Cell["\<\
PadicNormalize[r,p] removes the valuation from an exact rational r, giving r \
p^(-PadicOrder[r,p]); zero stays zero. PadicNormalize[s] sets a nonzero \
p-adic object's valuation to zero while retaining its unit digits and \
relative precision. For PadicLazy it retains a recipe for the normalized \
value. It leaves exact and uncertain zero objects unchanged.\
\>", "Usage",ExpressionUUID->"fcaef7f9-b73c-40a0-8a1f-7532c98496d3"],

Cell["\<\
For a nonzero canonical p-adic object, normalization sets the valuation to \
zero while retaining its unit digits and relative precision. This changes its \
value and its absolute precision.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"a626451e-73de-4581-b460-fdb7417bed7e"],

Cell["\<\
PadicCanonicalize instead preserves the represented value and, for an \
approximation, its absolute precision. Zero objects are unchanged by \
PadicNormalize.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"87eaabe3-27c7-4aa1-90d5-c6513320bf9e"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"Needs", "[", "\"\<PadicNumbers`\>\"", 
  "]"}]], "ExampleInitialization",ExpressionUUID->"8143c16b-f368-49e9-8729-\
ea8c7f3ecb1c"],

Cell["Basic Examples", "PrimaryExamplesSection",ExpressionUUID->"f6952a42-2a7a-44e5-9b9e-b34e4be8c503"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

